# =============================================================================
# Merge strategy for append-prone, machine-edited files
# -----------------------------------------------------------------------------
# `_data/backlog.yml` is the autopilot's shared to-do list. Every parallel
# content run appends a fresh item to the *end* of the same file, so two runs
# branched off the same `main` reliably collide on those final lines — the
# recurring "the one file the whole fleet fights over" merge conflict.
#
# THIS USED TO BE `merge=union`, AND UNION WAS WRONG HERE. Union resolves an
# append/append collision by keeping BOTH sides' lines, which is right only when
# the two appends share no lines. Two backlog items written by the same persona
# share byte-identical middle lines (`voice:`, `author:`, `priority: P2`,
# `status: done`); the diff aligns those as common context, splitting one clean
# append/append hunk into several small ones, and the union of THOSE interleaves
# the two items rather than stacking them. On PR #453 the incoming item lost its
# `voice/author/priority/status` and its `published:` line was stranded inside
# the PREVIOUS item's mapping — a duplicate key that Psych resolves silently, so
# the file still parsed and the breakage survived 13 pipeline runs. Union also
# stacked two items claiming the SAME id, which is a collision, not a merge.
#
# `merge=backlog` is `scripts/ci/merge_backlog.rb`: the same 3-way merge done
# per ITEM BLOCK keyed by id, on raw lines (comments and formatting survive
# byte-for-byte). A block is atomic, so two items can never be spliced together
# however many lines they share, and a real collision — the same new id on both
# sides, or one item edited differently on both sides — exits non-zero so git
# reports an honest conflict instead of a mangled file.
#
# IMPORTANT, AND UNCHANGED FROM THE UNION ERA: merge drivers only take effect
# when a REAL `git merge`/`git rebase` runs. GitHub's mergeability check and the
# green merge button DO NOT run them, so on GitHub a colliding PR still shows
# `dirty` and auto-merge skips it. The resolution is applied in a runner by
# `.github/workflows/auto-update.yml`, which merges main into each open content
# PR and pushes the result so GitHub then sees the branch as genuinely
# mergeable. Unlike `union`, a custom driver needs to be registered in git
# config; that workflow does it, and anywhere it is NOT registered git falls
# back to the normal text merge — a conflict, which is the safe direction.
#
# Scope any merge driver narrowly — never apply one to prose, and never apply a
# line-based one (like `union`) to structured config. `lint_artifacts.rb` reads
# this file to decide which data files to scan for duplicate mapping keys, so an
# entry here is also what puts a file under that guard.
# =============================================================================
_data/backlog.yml merge=backlog
